{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' docs/design/yang.preludes.md | nl -ba\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: Configurable and composable preludes for Yang language frontends — decomposing shared Layer-2a runtime building blocks (yrt.*) and decoupling frontend lowering from language runtimes via portable semantics descriptors.\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# yang.preludes: configurable and composable preludes for Yang frontends\n     6\t\n     7\t**Status:** Proposed design, 2026-11-20. No implementation or completed acceptance gate is claimed.\n     8\t\n     9\t**Authority:** Subordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md) (axioms, invariants, streams, host boundaries), [`yang.antlr.md`](./yang.antlr.md) (§3 frontend SPI, §8.1–§8.2 semantic placement, §8.5.5 float addresses, §8.5.6 linked modules, §8.10 shared calling convention, §8.11 cells, §9 four-layer stdlib model), [`yang.typescript.md`](./yang.typescript.md) (§2.2 shared rulings, §17 lift enumeration, §19 owner decision A12.5), [`yang.php.md`](./yang.php.md) (§9 prelude inventory), [`yin.vm.linker.md`](./yin.vm.linker.md), [`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md), and [`dao.jing.cbor.md`](./dao.jing.cbor.md).\n    10\t\n    11\t**Scope and amendments:** This document settles owner decision A12.5 of `yang.typescript.md` §19 (the deferred `yrt` shared prelude lift) and extends `yang.antlr.md` §3.1/§3.4/§9.3 to treat preludes as **composable runtime modules** and **swappable frontend libraries** (enabling, for example, `yang.python` syntax compiled against a `clojure.core` semantics prelude). It adds no UAST node type, no VM opcode, no manifest schema version, and no closed language registry.\n    12\t\n    13\t---\n    14\t\n    15\t## 1. Problem statement and repository baseline\n    16\t\n    17\t### 1.1 Current repository baseline\n    18\t\n    19\tTwo sharing mechanisms already exist in `src/cljc/yang/`:\n    20\t\n    21\t1. **Host-side emitter and admission machinery (`yang.core.*`):**\n    22\t   - `yang.core.prelude` (`src/cljc/yang/core/prelude.cljc`): pure UAST builders and symbol strippers (`define`, `strip`, `module-definitions`, `wide-app`, `module-tree`, `exports`, `free-names`, `defined-keys`, `free-name-vec`, `definitions->uast`, `balanced-definitions->uast`).\n    23\t   - `yang.core.prelude-admission` (`src/cljc/yang/core/prelude_admission.cljc`): `host-declaration`, `module-spec`, and `admit` over `yin.vm` and `yin.vm.module`.\n    24\t   - `yang.core.module` and `yang.core.publication`: shared linker module naming, free-primitive declaration, SCC bundle/alias specs, and dependency-first publication.\n    25\t   - *(Residual host helper duplication:)* the ClojureDart AOT `defquoted` macro is still duplicated across `yang.python.antlr.prelude`, `yang.typescript.antlr.prelude`, and `yang.php.antlr.quoted`.\n    26\t2. **One shared guest runtime module (`yang.core.call`):**\n    27\t   - `src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc` emits the language-neutral calling convention (`yang.call`) using `yang.core.prelude` and `yang.core.prelude-admission`, with both a bundled tree (`uast`) and a linked module (`module-uast`, `module-spec`). All four frontends (`py`, `js`, `php`, `clj`) compose against it.\n    28\t\n    29\tDespite that shared plumbing, all remaining guest runtime code lives in four separate language preludes and three safepoint hook preludes:\n    30\t\n    31\t| Prelude namespace | Linker module | Size / definitions | Lowering coupling |\n    32\t|---|---|---|---|\n    33\t| `yang.python.antlr.prelude` | `py` | 4,188 LOC / 343 defs | `yang.python.antlr.lower` hardcodes 103 `py/*` names + `{:py/str}`, `{:py/float}`, `:py/None`, `:py/unbound` |\n    34\t| `yang.typescript.antlr.prelude` | `js` | 4,447 LOC / 475 defs | `yang.typescript.antlr.lower` hardcodes 93 `js/*` names + `:js/undefined`, `:js/null`, `:js/tdz`, `{:js/str}` |\n    35\t| `yang.php.antlr.prelude` (+ `_module`, `_p9`, `builtins`) | `php` | 8,150 LOC / 936 defs | `yang.php.antlr.lower` hardcodes 188 `php/*` names + `:php/null`, `{:php/type :array}`, etc. |\n    36\t| `yang.clojure.prelude` (+ inline family in `yang.clojure`) | `clj` | 250 LOC / 17 defs (+ `library`) | `yang.clojure` emits `clj/*` continuations and `yang.call/*` directly |\n    37\t| `yang.*.antlr.safepoint` | `pysp`, `jssp`, `php.sp` | 478 / 235 / 70 LOC | Hardcoded hook profiles |\n    38\t\n    39\t### 1.2 Two problems to solve together\n    40\t\n    41\t1. **Duplicated guest runtime mechanics:** ~1,200–2,000 lines of guest UAST across `py`, `js`, `php`, and `clj` implement the exact same algorithms under language-prefixed names: the `%capture` handler-stack state machine, IEEE-754 float64 math (`fmod-pos` Sterbenz loop, integer-exponent `fpow` with split-half reciprocal `neg-power`, `nan`/`inf`/`-0.0`, `float-floor` binary descent, `float-parts`, decimal layout over `integer/float-digits`), fixed-width integer wrapping and radix digit scanning, `gen-switch` coroutine stepping, and `stream/poll!` signal safepoint hooks.\n    42\t2. **Hardcoded syntax-to-prelude coupling:** A frontend lowering currently assumes one fixed runtime profile. Even though `yang.frontend` (`src/cljc/yang/frontend.cljc`) already tracks `:yang.frontend/grammar`, `:yang.frontend/lowering-profile`, `:yang.frontend/runtime-profile`, and `:yang.frontend/support-profile` independently in its manifest, a lowering cannot today treat its target prelude as a configurable library—such as lowering Python syntax directly onto `clojure.core` and persistent Yin values.\n    43\t\n    44\tSeparating these two concerns while unifying their manifest model allows both **shared guest runtime reuse** (`yrt.*`) and **pluggable semantics preludes** without touching the VM kernel or moving default language goldens prematurely.\n    45\t\n    46\t---\n    47\t\n    48\t## 2. Derivation from foundations and non-negotiable invariants\n    49\t\n    50\tThis design is constrained directly by `docs/design/datom.world.md` and `src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md`:\n    51\t\n    52\t### 2.1 Foundational axioms\n    53\t\n    54\t1. **Everything is a Stream:** Prelude selection, composition, and lowering are pure functions of explicit data configurations and CST streams. Any external effect a prelude needs (`stream/put!`, `stream/poll!`, host I/O) remains a declared Layer-4 effect in the module manifest; prelude composition adds no ambient I/O.\n    55\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics:** A CST packet carries syntax only; its runtime meaning emerges from the tuple `(cst-packet, lowering-profile, semantics-descriptor, runtime-profile)`. Interpreting one Python CST under `:yang.python/py-v1` (CPython semantics) versus `:yang.python/clojure-core-v1` (Clojure persistent-data semantics) is a direct realization of \"one truth, many perspectives.\"\n    56\t3. **Code and State are Datoms (and Unitarity / Derive-Don't-Persist):**\n    57\t   - Lowering preserves source CST identity; compiled UAST roots under different preludes have distinct content addresses linked by explicit provenance datoms pinning `(cst-address, lowering-profile, semantics-descriptor, runtime-profile)`.\n    58\t   - Zero new AST tags, zero VM opcodes, and zero manifest schema changes are introduced. Linker manifests retain schema 1, and C1 `:yang/call-site` tables continue to publish beside manifests rather than inside them.\n    59\t   - Composition facts (which `yrt.*` modules a prelude requires) live in the explicit module `:requires` DAG, never as a second redundant tag in UAST rows.\n    60\t4. **Everything is a Continuation:** Shared control-flow primitives (`yrt.escape`) and coroutine stepping (`yrt.gen`) remain pure guest UAST over `%capture` and task-heap cells (`cell/*`).\n    61\t\n    62\t### 2.2 Non-negotiable invariants\n    63\t\n    64\t1. **No hidden global state — stateless `yrt.*` modules:**\n    65\t   - `yang.core.*` holds no mutable global registry of preludes or languages (`yang.frontend` immutable catalog rule).\n    66\t   - **Shared `yrt.*` modules own zero `*.rt/*` state cells.** Every stateful `yrt.*` function takes the caller's task-local state cell (e.g., `ctx-cell`) as an explicit parameter. When a single Yin task links `py`, `js`, `php`, and `clj` modules together via `yang.call`, each language maintains its own isolated handler stack and depth/base accounting inside its own `*.rt/ctx` cell rather than aliasing or corrupting another language's stack.\n    67\t2. **No implicit control flow:** All escapes remain explicit continuation invocations over an explicit handler chain.\n    68\t3. **No raw callbacks:** Policy hooks passed by a language prelude into `yrt.*` (`on-empty`, `on-range`, `on-bit-limit`, `on-digit-limit`, `fin`) are ordinary guest Yin closures, never host-boundary callbacks.\n    69\t4. **No shared mutable state:** Each language profile allocates its own task-local cells once per task in its own `init!` under its own private namespace (`py.rt/*`, `js.rt/*`, `php.rt/*`, `clj.rt/*`).\n    70\t5. **No layer collapsing:** Parsing (host ANTLR boundary) → lowering (syntax + semantics descriptor → UAST) → prelude composition & admission (pure spec construction; never publishes or executes) → publication/linking → VM execution stay separated across explicit stream/module boundaries.\n    71\t6. **No assumed graphs:** Every composed prelude declares an explicit, acyclic `:requires` DAG of content addresses. Missing exports or cycles fail closed at admission time (`yang.core.prelude-admission`) before publication or execution.\n    72\t7. **No closed language registry (`yang.antlr.md` owner directive):** Neither `yrt.*` nor `yang.core.*` contains a `case`/dispatch over `:python`, `:typescript`, `:php`, or `:clojure`, nor inspects `:py/type`, `:js/type`, or `:php/type` tags. All parameterization is structural or closure-supplied.\n    73\t8. **Security boundary — VM provenance doors stay private:** Under `src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md` and `src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc`, `cell/dsg__new-provenance` and `cell/dsg__provenance` are reserved compiler/runtime-module doors whose names guest code must not forge or re-export. **`yrt.*` modules MUST NEVER wrap, mint, read, or export provenance-door primitives.** Retained protocol-condition provenance (`yang.antlr.md` §8.10.7) stays strictly inside each trusted runtime module's private exception boundary.\n    74\t\n    75\t---\n    76\t\n    77\t## 3. Three-layer prelude architecture\n    78\t\n    79\t`yang.antlr.md` §9.1 Layer 2 (\"Language intrinsics and preludes\") is decomposed into three explicit sub-layers, all expressed as ordinary Universal AST over existing host modules (`cell`, `data`, `integer`, `stream`, `kind`, `portable`):\n    80\t\n    81\t```text\n    82\t+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    83\t| Layer 2c: Ambient & Standard Libraries (e.g., clojure.core, py.builtins)    |\n    84\t|   Ordinary compiled linker modules projected into scope via yang.call       |\n    85\t+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    86\t                                      ^\n    87\t                                      | binds / lowers through\n    88\t+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    89\t| Layer 2b: Semantics-Protocol Libraries (e.g., py, js, php, clj, clj.py)     |\n    90\t|   Language value encodings, coercions, error types, state cells, init!      |\n    91\t+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    92\t                                      ^\n    93\t                                      | composes explicitly via :requires DAG\n    94\t+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    95\t| Layer 2a: Composable Runtime Building Blocks (yang.call, yrt.*)             |\n    96\t|   Stateless, language-neutral UAST: yang.call, yrt.escape, yrt.float,       |\n    97\t|   yrt.int, yrt.gen, yrt.safepoint                                           |\n    98\t+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    99\t                                      ^\n   100\t                                      | host-declared pure/effect primitives\n   101\t+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   102\t| Host Registry Modules: cell | data | integer | stream | (kind, portable)    |\n   103\t+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   104\t```\n   105\t\n   106\t### 3.1 Explicit exclusions from Layer 2a (`yrt.*`)\n   107\t\n   108\tNot every similar-looking mechanism belongs in `yrt.*`. The following remain in Layer 2b (per-language preludes) because unifying them either breaks semantic fidelity or forces a closed type-tag dispatch:\n   109\t\n   110\t| Mechanism | Why it stays in Layer 2b |\n   111\t|---|---|\n   112\t| **Ordered maps / dicts / arrays** | Python dicts normalize `1 == 1.0 == True` via exact rationals and tuple-key recursion; JS own-key maps sort uint32 indices ahead of strings and symbols; PHP arrays coerce int64-looking strings, track `:next-index`, and implement copy-on-write refcounted handles. Only the core storage idioms (`yin.vm.data`) are shared. |\n   113\t| **Module / import loaders** | Python `sys.modules` + package hierarchy, JS ESM link/eval stacks + live bindings, and PHP dynamic `include`/`require` scope-cell adoption have incompatible lifecycle rules. |\n   114\t| **Object / class / prototype dispatch** | MRO/descriptors (`py`), prototype chains/accessors (`js`), and traits/refcounted destructors (`php`) belong to `yang.antlr.md` §9.4 Layer 3. |\n   115\t| **Provenance doors** | `cell/dsg__new-provenance` and `cell/dsg__provenance` are security-sensitive VM doors (`§2.2` invariant 8). |\n   116\t| **PHP `php.sp` budget/ticks hooks** | `php.sp` uses a decrementing execution-budget cell (`php.sp/remaining`) and `:line :after` statement ticks rather than `stream/poll!` signal cursors. |\n   117\t\n   118\t---\n   119\t\n   120\t## 4. Layer 2a: composable `yrt.*` module specifications\n   121\t\n   122\tEvery Layer-2a module follows the single-source dual-emitter pattern already established by `yang.core.call`: one ordered definition vector emitting both a bundled `uast` (tail-marked) and a linked `module-uast` + `module-spec` (wide-app over stripped definitions, C1-finalized call sites, and `prelude-admission/module-spec`).\n   123\t\n   124\t### 4.1 `yang.call` *(existing)*\n   125\t\n   126\tRemains unchanged at `src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc` (`yang.antlr.md` §8.10): structural callable discovery (`entry`, `eligible?`), invocation records (`invocation`, `invocation-with`, `valid-invocation?`), and condition transfer (`condition`, `thrown`, `transfer`, `invoke`, `invoke-with`). Requires no `yrt.*` module so `yang.call` stays a leaf dependency.\n   127\t\n   128\t### 4.2 `yrt.escape` — `%capture` handler stack and escapes\n   129\t\n   130\tUsed by: `py`, `js`, `php`, `clj`. Host dependencies: `cell/get`, `cell/set!`, `cell/new`.\n   131\t\n   132\tReconciles the current per-language divergences (`4-slot` vs `5-slot` frames, `:base` rebasing, `orelse` vs value-return, PHP `abandon-to`, normal-exit `finally` sentinel, and empty-stack handling) by parameterizing `ctx-cell`, `normal-arg`, and `on-empty`:\n   133\t\n   134\t- **Context record:** `{:handlers hs :depth d :base b :frame f}` (`js` and `clj` initialize `:depth 0 :base 0 :frame nil`).\n   135\t- **Frame tuple:** 5 elements `[kind payload rest n depth]`, where `kind` is `:handler` or `:finally`, `n` is frame count `(+ 1 (frame-depth rest))`, and `depth` is the activation-relative call depth at push time.\n   136\t- **`restore!` invariant:** `(fn [ctx-cell saved] (cell/set! ctx-cell (assoc saved :base (get (cell/get ctx-cell) :base))))` — always preserves the activation's current `:base` across generator crossings.\n   137\t\n   138\t**Public exports:**\n   139\t\n   140\t| Export | Signature | Behavior |\n   141\t|---|---|---|\n   142\t| `ctx` | `[handlers depth base frame]` | Construct context record |\n   143\t| `handlers` / `set-handlers!` / `restore!` | `[ctx-cell ...]` | Context cell accessors/mutators |\n   144\t| `frame-depth` / `frame` / `pop-frame` | `[ctx-cell ...]` | Frame stack manipulation (restores `:handlers` and `:depth`) |\n   145\t| `raise` | `[ctx-cell e on-empty]` | Unwind `:finally` frames passing `e`; invoke innermost `:handler`; call `(on-empty e)` if stack is empty (`py`/`js`/`clj` return a sentinel or throw; `php` triggers `invariant-violation`) |\n   146\t| `unwind-to` | `[ctx-cell n normal-arg]` | Pop frames above depth `n`, running each `:finally` thunk with `normal-arg` (`:py/None`, `:js/undefined`, `:php/null`, `nil`) |\n   147\t| `abandon-to` | `[ctx-cell n]` | Pop frames above depth `n` **without** running `:finally` thunks (PHP terminal fatals / `exit`) |\n   148\t| `try-orelse` | `[ctx-cell body handler orelse]` | Install `:handler`; on normal exit restore and tail-call `(orelse)`; on escape restore and call `(handler r)` (`py`, `php`) |\n   149\t| `try-value` | `[ctx-cell body handler]` | Install `:handler`; on normal exit restore and return `(body)`'s value directly (`js`, `clj`) |\n   150\t| `try-finally` | `[ctx-cell body fin normal-arg]` | Install `:finally`; on normal exit restore, run `(fin normal-arg)`, return `v` |\n   151\t| `call-ec` | `[ctx-cell f normal-arg]` | Capture escape continuation that runs `unwind-to` then `restore!` before invoking `r` |\n   152\t| `call-terminal` | `[ctx-cell f]` | Capture terminal escape that runs `abandon-to` then `restore!` before invoking `r` (`php`) |\n   153\t\n   154\t**Capture-safety for bundled `yang.clojure`:** When `yang.clojure` runs in non-linked (`:linked-runtime false`) mode, user code can shadow `get`, `=`, `assoc`, `empty?`, `conj`, `+`, `<=`. Just as `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2472` wraps `call/uast` in `(u/app (u/lam '[get = assoc empty?] ...) dsg__...)`, `yrt.escape/bundled-uast` is wrapped in an outer lambda binding those primitives to the compiler's `dsg__*` anti-shadowing helpers. In linked mode, module lexical isolation makes the wrapper unnecessary.\n   155\t\n   156\t### 4.3 `yrt.float` — IEEE-754 float64 math seam\n   157\t\n   158\tUsed by: `py`, `js`, `php` (omitted by `clj`). Host dependencies: `data/float64`, `data/float-value`, `data/content=`, `data/count`, `data/nth`, `data/str-concat`, `integer/shift-right`, `integer/bit-and`, `integer/sub`, `integer/floor-div-mod`, `integer/float-digits`, `integer/decimal->float`.\n   159\t\n   160\t**Representational contract:**\n   161\t- `yrt.float` operates purely on **unwrapped host doubles** (`data/float-value` outputs) for arithmetic and **untagged `data/float64` carriers** where boxed values are needed. It never inspects or emits `{:py/float ...}` tags; `py` wraps/unwraps at its thin arithmetic entry until/unless Python floats are ever untagged (owner decision D3).\n   162\t- Preserves all `yang.antlr.md` §8.5.5 float-address and ClojureDart rules: zero integral float literals (`(data/float-value 1)` instead of `1.0`), `-0.0` constructed as `(* (data/float-value -1) (data/float-value 0))`.\n   163\t\n   164\t**Public exports:**\n   165\t- **Constants & predicates:** `inf`, `neg-inf`, `nan`, `zero`, `neg-zero`, `nan?` (`(not (<= x x))`), `finite?` (`(< (abs x) inf)`), `zero?` (`(if (= x zero) true false)` via safe double comparison), `neg-zero?`, `abs`, `neg`, `zero-like` (`copysign(0.0, y)`).\n   166\t- **Exact IEEE-754 operations:**\n   167\t  - `fdiv` — IEEE division + zero-sign / `0/0 -> NaN` / `Inf` handling.\n   168\t  - `fmod-pos` — the exact Sterbenz long-division loop for `x >= 0, y > 0` (currently character-identical in `py`, `js`, and `php`).\n   169\t  - `fpow` (`[base e]`, integer exponent `e >= 0` by squaring) and `neg-pow` (`[x n on-underflow]`, split-half reciprocal handling `2^-1074` without premature overflow, invoking `(on-underflow)` when `p` is zero).\n   170\t  - `floor-int` (`[x on-range]`, binary descent via `pow2-above` + `floor-descend` for `|x| < 2^53`, calling `on-range` outside range) and `float-floor`.\n   171\t  - `float-parts` — exact `(mantissa, exponent)` decomposition.\n   172\t  - `zeros`, `exp-text`, and shortest-round-trip decimal digit formatting primitives over `integer/float-digits` (each language supplies its own exponent threshold and `NaN`/`Inf` spelling).\n   173\t\n   174\t### 4.4 `yrt.int` — exact-integer helpers and fixed-width wrapping\n   175\t\n   176\tUsed by: `py`, `js`, `php` (omitted by `clj`). Host dependencies: `data/count`, `data/nth`, `integer/*`.\n   177\t\n   178\t**Public exports:**\n   179\t- **Fixed-width wrapping:** `wrap-unsigned` (`[x width-pow2 mask]`) and `wrap-signed` (`[x half-range range]`), unifying `js/wrap32` / `js/to-int32` / `js/to-uint32` and `php/wrap64`; `in-range?` (`[x min-v max-v]`).\n   180\t- **Digit & radix scanners:** `digit-value` (`[code-point]`), `all-digits?` (`[cs i n radix]`), `scan-digits` (`[cs i n radix acc]`), `skip-ws`, `skip-zeros`.\n   181\t- **Limit-breach outcome adapter:** `unwrap-result` (`[res on-bit-limit on-digit-limit on-refusal]`), inspecting `yin.vm.integer` v4 structured refusals (`:yin.vm.integer/bit-limit`, `:yin.vm.integer/digit-limit`) so `py` can raise `MemoryError`/`ValueError`, `js` `RangeError`, and `php` `Error` without duplicating refusal inspection.\n   182\t- **Admission ownership:** Per-language integer limits (`min-integer-bits = 53` for `py`/`js`; `min-integer-bits = 4096, min-integer-digits = 1000` for `php`) stay enforced by each language profile's admission step, not hardcoded inside `yrt.int`.\n   183\t\n   184\t### 4.5 `yrt.gen` and `yrt.safepoint` *(Tier-2 shared components)*\n   185\t\n   186\tDeferred to Slice P4 (after core + math lands) to bound initial migration scope:\n   187\t- **`yrt.gen` (`py`, `php`, future `js`):** Extracts the `gen-switch` coroutine resume/throw/close frame switching and `ctx-cell` `:base` rebasing (`resumer-abs-depth + 1`), parameterized by guest lifecycle closures (`on-enter`, `on-exit`, `on-throw`, `on-close`) so PHP's refcounted value retention (`addref`/`release`) and Python's `RecursionError` frame-fit checks remain language-owned.\n   188\t- **`yrt.safepoint` (`pysp`, `jssp`):** Extracts `stream/cursor`, `stream/poll!` gap/signal detection, and `attach!` setup into a shared module (`yrt.safepoint`), leaving signal delivery policy (`KeyboardInterrupt` / registered signal handler in `pysp`; uncatchable root `Interrupt` unwind in `jssp`) and recursion depth counting (`pysp`) in each hook module.\n   189\t\n   190\t---\n   191\t\n   192\t## 5. Declarative prelude composition and admission\n   193\t\n   194\tTo eliminate ad-hoc `module-spec` and `admit` boilerplate while preserving deterministic content addresses, every runtime prelude is described by a pure, portable EDN profile value (`:yang.prelude/profile`) that satisfies `yang.frontend/non-portable-path` (zero host functions or closures inside the profile map).\n   195\t\n   196\t### 5.1 Portable `:yang.prelude/profile` schema\n   197\t\n   198\t```clojure\n   199\t{:yang.prelude/schema 1\n   200\t :yang.prelude/id :yang.python/py-prelude\n   201\t :yang.prelude/revision \"2026-11-20.1\"\n   202\t :yang.prelude/module 'py\n   203\t :yang.prelude/components\n   204\t [[yang.call \"<blake3-address>\"]\n   205\t  [yrt.escape \"<blake3-address>\"]\n   206\t  [yrt.float \"<blake3-address>\"]\n   207\t  [yrt.int \"<blake3-address>\"]]\n   208\t :yang.prelude/host-modules\n   209\t {:cell {:required true}\n   210\t  :data {:required true, :min-max-items 1048576}\n   211\t  :integer {:required true, :min-bits 53}}\n   212\t :yang.prelude/emitter\n   213\t {:mode :linear                  ;; :linear (definitions->uast) | :balanced (balanced-definitions->uast)\n   214\t  :uninit :py/uninit\n   215\t  :rt-namespaces #{\"py.rt\" \"py.b\"}\n   216\t  :unshadow-locals? false\n   217\t  :capture-safe-primitives nil}  ;; optional primitive list for bundled capture-safety (e.g. clj)\n   218\t :yang.prelude/effects #{}       ;; declared VM effect keywords (e.g. #{:stream/put})\n   219\t :yang.prelude/refusal-ns \"yang.python.antlr\"}\n   220\t```\n   221\t\n   222\t### 5.2 Composition rules and admission checks\n   223\t\n   224\t`yang.core.prelude` and `yang.core.prelude-admission` compose profiles uniformly for both execution modes:\n   225\t\n   226\t1. **Acyclic component DAG & disjoint export check (fail-closed):**\n   227\t   - `prelude-admission/validate-profile!` checks that `:yang.prelude/components` forms a valid topological order with no duplicate module symbols, no cycles, and no export collisions across components (or with `yin.vm/primitives` unless explicitly permitted).\n   228\t   - No `yrt.*` module may export or name `cell/dsg__new-provenance` or `cell/dsg__provenance` (`§2.2` invariant 8).\n   229\t2. **Host-module and limit admission (`admit-profile`):**\n   230\t   - Verifies that the host registry supplies every required host export (`prelude-admission/admit`) and satisfies every declared floor (`:min-bits`, `:min-digits`, `:min-max-items`) before any unit publishes or runs.\n   231\t3. **Linked emission (`module-spec`):**\n   232\t   - Derives `:requires` directly from `:yang.prelude/components` (`(into {} components)`) and `:skip?` from `(contains? component-module-names (symbol (namespace sym)))`.\n   233\t   - Emits `require` nodes for each component in `:yang.prelude/components` order inside `core/module-tree`, strips only the module's own namespace (preserving `:rt-namespaces` like `py.rt`, `js.rt`, `php.rt`, `clj.rt`), optionally unshadows locals that match stripped exports (`php`), and runs C1 call-site annotation (`:yang/call-site` published beside the schema-1 manifest).\n   234\t4. **Bundled emission (`compose-bundled`):**\n   235\t   - Concatenates component definition lists in topological order ahead of the profile's own `definitions` (and optional `init-form`), applying the declared `:mode` (`:linear` vs `:balanced`) and optional `:capture-safe-primitives` wrapper (`clj`).\n   236\t   - **Content-address guardrail (from commit `1604aabe`):** `:mode` (`:linear` vs `:balanced`) is an explicit pinned field of the profile; tooling must never silently migrate a profile between linear and balanced emission because tree shape alters the content address.\n   237\t5. **Bundled vs. Linked Parity Law:**\n   238\t   - Any program compiled under a composed prelude MUST produce identical outcomes in bundled mode and linked mode across the semantic, stack, and register VMs (and the AST walker once L-b lands).\n   239\t\n   240\t---\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
