{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'v4|bit-limit|digit-limit|refus|defn.*(wrap|parse)|integer-profiles' src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc | head -70; sed -n '4053,4188p' src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc; sed -n '6210,6245p' src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc; sed -n '6270,6375p' src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"23:   module keeps only the argument checks and refusals. Under a\n78:   would breach `::max-bits` is refused before it is built wherever its\n82:   the keyword `::bit-limit` or `::digit-limit` in place of its result,\n86:   answered with one half refused. A guest prelude translates the three\n88:   `::bit-limit` to `MemoryError` and `::digit-limit` to `ValueError`,\n93:   Every other refusal throws `ex-info` with the message\n94:   `refusal-message` and ex-data built here, never host exception text,\n126:(def refusal-message\n127:  \"The message of every refusal an `integer` export throws.\"\n128:  \"integer primitive refused\")\n131:(defn- refuse!\n133:  (throw (ex-info refusal-message (merge {::op op, ::reason reason} data))))\n139:  (refuse! op :wrong-type {::arg arg, ::expected expected}))\n153:   `:wrong-type` refusal.\"\n171:  \"Big integer result `b` in its canonical carrier, or `::bit-limit`.\"\n174:    ::bit-limit\n211:      ::bit-limit\n281:      (refuse! op :zero-division {}))\n310:      (neg? (sign n)) (refuse! 'pow :negative-exponent {})\n316:      (pos? (host/compare-big n (host/from-native max-bits))) ::bit-limit\n317:      (> (inc (* (dec (bits x)) (host/to-native n))) max-bits) ::bit-limit\n359:   refusal.\"\n363:      (refuse! op :negative-count {}))\n375:      (pos? (host/compare-big n (host/from-native max-bits))) ::bit-limit\n376:      (> (+ (bits x) (host/to-native n)) max-bits) ::bit-limit\n430:  \"Argument `arg` of `op` as a host double, or a `:wrong-type` refusal.\"\n481:      (refuse! op :out-of-range {::arg 1, ::lo 2, ::hi 36}))\n491:  \"The value of each digit of `s` in radix `r`, or a `:syntax` refusal\n498:              (refuse! op :syntax {}))))\n502:(defn- int-parse\n512:       (refuse! 'parse :syntax {}))\n514:       ::digit-limit\n535:     ;; digits: refuse before building text that is certain to breach\n539:       ::digit-limit\n543:           ::digit-limit\n565:      (refuse! 'decimal->float :syntax {}))\n624:  \"`f` refusing, as data, a call whose argument count `arities` does not\n631:          (refuse! op :arity {::argc n}))\n663:(def integer-profiles\n677:                               integer-profiles))\n(def ^:private definitions->uast core/definitions->uast)\n\n\n(def functions-uast\n  \"The shared call runtime followed by every Python definition. Defining\n   them runs nothing, so this loads on any composition, with or without cells.\"\n  (definitions->uast (into call/definitions definitions)))\n\n\n(def uast\n  \"The bundled prelude: every definition, then the task's runtime state\n   allocated by `py/init!`.\"\n  (u/then functions-uast (u/sexp->uast '(py/init!))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The module emitter: the same definition list as the linker module `py`\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def module-name\n  \"The linker module the Python runtime profile is published as.\"\n  'py)\n\n\n(def runtime-keys\n  \"Every key `py/init!` allocates, the ready flag aside, sorted: the four\n   runtime cells, the builtin classes, functions and method\n   implementations, the builtins dict, and sys.modules with its three\n   pre-seeded module objects. Read from the same tables `init-form` reads.\"\n  (vec (sort-by str\n                (concat '[py.rt/ctx py.rt/limit py.rt/out py.rt/value py.b/builtins\n                          py.rt/modules py.rt/sys py.rt/builtins-mod py.rt/importlib]\n                        (mapv second builtin-classes)\n                        (mapv second builtin-functions)\n                        (mapv first method-implementations)))))\n\n\n(defn strip\n  \"`sym` without the module's own namespace `py`; any other value as it\n   is. `py.b/*` and `py.rt/*` stay qualified: they are internal store keys,\n   and stripping them would collide (`py/list` and `py.b/list`).\"\n  [sym]\n  (core/strip \"py\" sym))\n\n\n(def ^:private module-definitions\n  \"The module's definition list: a `:py/uninit` placeholder for every\n   runtime key, so each read of one is discharged by an unconditional\n   module-level definition, then every definition, stripped.\"\n  (core/module-definitions \"py\" :py/uninit runtime-keys definitions))\n\n\n(def module-uast\n  \"The module tree: one application whose operands are the definitions,\n   in order, followed by `require yang.call`, applied to a lambda that\n   returns nil. Every definition runs on the main sequence before the\n   require, so the linker's scanners discharge every body read of a sibling\n   or runtime key. Defining runs nothing.\"\n  (core/module-tree module-definitions\n                    [(u/sexp->uast '(require (quote yang.call)))]))\n\n\n(def module-exports\n  \"The module's exports: every `py/` key of the definition list,\n   stripped. The runtime keys and the state slot are internal.\"\n  (core/exports \"py\" definitions))\n\n\n(def free-names\n  \"Every variable name some occurrence of the map AST reads unbound by an\n   enclosing lambda.\"\n  core/free-names)\n\n\n(def defined-keys\n  \"Every literal key the map AST defines with `yin/def`, at any depth.\"\n  core/defined-keys)\n\n\n(def ^:private module-free-names\n  \"The names the module tree reads and does not define, sorted: the\n   primitives and the host exports its manifest must declare.\"\n  (core/free-name-vec module-uast))\n\n\n(defn module-spec\n  \"`yin.vm.linker.publish/publish-module!`'s spec for the module `py`\n   over the host registry `registry`. Every free name of the module tree\n   is declared under `:primitives`; a free name nothing supplies is\n   refused before anything is published. This namespace emits; it never\n   publishes.\"\n  [registry call-address]\n  (prelude-admission/module-spec\n    {:module-name module-name,\n     :ast module-uast,\n     :exports module-exports,\n     :requires {'yang.call call-address},\n     :free-names module-free-names,\n     :skip? #(= \"yang.call\" (namespace %)),\n     :registry registry,\n     :message \"the py module reads an undeclared name\",\n     :refusal-ns \"yang.python.antlr\"}))\n\n\n(def min-integer-bits\n  \"The smallest `::integer/max-bits` this profile admits: every inline\n   literal (up to 2^53 - 1) must fit (C3 S2).\"\n  53)\n\n\n(defn register-integer-module\n  \"Return `registry` with the `integer` module installed under `limits`\n   for this profile: refused when `::integer/max-bits` is under\n   `min-integer-bits`, otherwise `integer/register-integer-module`.\"\n  [registry limits]\n  (let [b (::integer/max-bits limits)]\n    (when-not (and (int? b) (<= min-integer-bits b))\n      (throw (ex-info \"the Python profile needs integer max-bits >= 53\"\n                      {:yang.python.antlr/refusal :yang.python.antlr/max-bits,\n                       ::integer/max-bits b}))))\n  (integer/register-integer-module registry limits))\n\n\n(defn admit\n  \"Return `registry` when it supplies every one of `host-names`, else\n   refuse it before any program runs, naming each missing export in\n   `:yang.python.antlr/missing`: a version-3 `integer` module (no\n   `float-digits`, `decimal->float`, `max-digits`) or a `data` module\n   registered without `:yin.vm.data/max-items` is a profile mismatch, not a\n   failure of the program that first calls the export. A Python\n   composition calls this after its registrars. Linking replaces it with\n   requirement discovery (yang.antlr.md 8.5.4).\"\n  [registry]\n  (prelude-admission/admit registry host-names\n                           \"the composition lacks host names the prelude calls\"\n                           \"yang.python.antlr\"))\n   runs nothing.\"\n  (into (into (into core-definitions module-defs/definitions) p9-defs/definitions)\n        [['php.rt/state :php/uninit]\n         ['php/init! (init-form)]]))\n\n\n(defn- definitions->uast\n  [defs]\n  (core/balanced-definitions->uast defs))\n\n\n(def functions-uast\n  \"The bundled shared-call definitions, PHP lambdas and literal state slot.\"\n  (definitions->uast (into call/definitions definitions)))\n\n\n(def uast\n  \"The bundled prelude: every definition, then the task's runtime state\n   allocated by `php/init!`.\"\n  (u/then functions-uast (u/sexp->uast '(php/init!))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The module emitter: the same definition list as the linker module `php`\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def module-name\n  \"The linker module the PHP runtime profile is published as.\"\n  'php)\n\n\n(def runtime-keys\n  \"Every key `php/init!` allocates, the ready flag aside, sorted.\"\n  '[php.rt/class-index php.rt/classes php.rt/cmp-depth php.rt/ctx php.rt/diag php.rt/diag-stream\n    php.rt/error-handler\n    php.rt/error-mask php.rt/error-masks\n    (and (seq? form) (= 'let (first form)))\n    (into (set (take-nth 2 (second form)))\n          (mapcat binder-symbols)\n          (concat (take-nth 2 (rest (second form))) (drop 2 form)))\n    (coll? form) (into #{} (mapcat binder-symbols) (seq form))\n    :else #{}))\n\n\n(defn- unshadow\n  \"`form` with every local binder named like a module export renamed (`h` to\n   `h%l`): the module emitter strips `php/h` to the bare `h`, which a local\n   `h` would otherwise capture. Globals are qualified before stripping, so\n   every bare occurrence is the local.\"\n  [exports [k form]]\n  (let [clash (set (filter exports (binder-symbols form)))]\n    [k\n     (if (empty? clash)\n       form\n       (walk/postwalk (fn [x] (if (contains? clash x) (symbol (str x \"%l\")) x)) form))]))\n\n\n(def ^:private module-definitions\n  (core/module-definitions\n    \"php\" :php/uninit runtime-keys\n    (mapv (partial unshadow (into #{} (map (fn [[k _]] (strip k))) definitions)) definitions)))\n\n\n(def module-uast\n  \"The module tree: one application whose operands are the definitions, in\n   order. Defining runs nothing.\"\n  (core/module-tree module-definitions []))\n\n\n(def module-exports\n  \"The module's exports: every `php/` key of the definition list, stripped.\"\n  (core/exports \"php\" definitions))\n\n\n(def free-names core/free-names)\n\n\n(def defined-keys core/defined-keys)\n\n\n(def ^:private module-free-names\n  (core/free-name-vec module-uast))\n\n\n(defn module-spec\n  \"`yin.vm.linker.publish/publish-module!`'s spec for the module `php` over\n   the host registry `registry`, requiring the pinned `yang.call` revision\n   at `call-address`; a free name nothing supplies is refused before\n   anything is published. This namespace emits; it never publishes.\"\n  [registry call-address]\n  (prelude-admission/module-spec\n    {:module-name module-name,\n     :ast module-uast,\n     :exports module-exports,\n     :requires {call/module-name call-address},\n     :free-names module-free-names,\n     :skip? #(= \"yang.call\" (namespace %)),\n     :registry registry,\n     :message \"the php module reads an undeclared name\",\n     :refusal-ns \"yang.php\"}))\n\n\n(def min-integer-bits\n  \"The smallest `::integer/max-bits` this profile admits: the exact float\n   expansion `m * 5^k` needs about 2200 bits.\"\n  4096)\n\n\n(def min-integer-digits\n  \"The smallest `::integer/max-digits` this profile admits: the exact float\n   expansion has up to about 770 digits.\"\n  1000)\n\n\n(defn register-integer-module\n  \"Return `registry` with the `integer` module installed under `limits`,\n   refused when they are under this profile's minimum.\"\n  [registry limits]\n  (let [b (::integer/max-bits limits)\n        d (::integer/max-digits limits)]\n    (when-not (and (int? b) (<= min-integer-bits b) (int? d) (<= min-integer-digits d))\n      (throw (ex-info \"the PHP profile needs integer max-bits >= 4096 and max-digits >= 1000\"\n                      {:yang.php/refusal :yang.php/limits,\n                       ::integer/max-bits b,\n                       ::integer/max-digits d}))))\n  (integer/register-integer-module registry limits))\n\n\n(defn admit\n  \"Return `registry` when it supplies every one of `host-names`, else refuse\n   it before any program runs, naming each missing export in\n   `:yang.php/missing`.\"\n  [registry]\n  (prelude-admission/admit registry host-names\n                           \"the composition lacks host names the prelude calls\"\n                           \"yang.php\"))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'prefix|overlap|dotted|collision' src/cljc/yin/vm/{module.cljc,linker*} docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md; sed -n '1,102p' src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc; sed -n '430,463p' src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc; sed -n '70,90p' src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc; cat src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/quoted.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'prefix|overlap|dotted|collision' src/cljc/yin/vm/{module.cljc,linker*} docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md; sed -n '1,102p' src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc; sed -n '430,463p' src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc; sed -n '70,90p' src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc; cat src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/quoted.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:34:  \"Split a dotted symbol into a get-in path of symbols.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:63:   `:modules` map), or nil when the dotted path reaches no module entry.\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:150:  \"Get a value from a registry value by dotted symbol path.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:231:   module-name is a dotted symbol; fns maps each export symbol to the host\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1262:   give each scheduler a distinct origin prefix as composition data;\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1263:   two schedulers on one pair with one prefix is a host assembly defect.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2999:|                                   | distinct prefixes (7.2 step 3).   |\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:338:   prefix of the path; `in-body?` is whether a lambda body encloses the\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc:3:   traces, each under its own domain-separation prefix.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc:30:;; A published head trace's prefix (yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md 5.2): domain\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc:87:  ([prefix canonical]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc:88:   (let [p-bytes (utf8 prefix)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/pair.cljc:650:   a raw prefix, so `:t0.10` is not a descendant of `:t0.1`.\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:701:(defn- sequenced-prefixes\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:702:  \"The path prefix of each of `n` programs inside `(sequenced asts)`:\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:710:          (sequenced-prefixes (dec n)))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:948:                                             (mapcat (fn [[prefix rows]]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:951:                                                               (into prefix p)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:955:                                                  (sequenced-prefixes\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head.cljc:38:     :reason r ...}`, `r` one of `:yin.head/seq-collision`,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head.cljc:214:   another manifest is refused `:yin.head/seq-collision`, a lower one\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head.cljc:231:      (refusal :yin.head/seq-collision {:manifest manifest :seq n\n(ns yin.vm.integer\n  \"The `integer` host module: exact-integer kernels a language prelude\n   writes its integer semantics over (C3 big-integer design, converged\n   rulings 1, 2, 5 and 11).\n\n   Every export is `:pure` with declared effects `#{}`. As with\n   `yin.vm.data`, the module is not part of `yin.vm/primitives`: a\n   composition whose runtime profile names it installs it explicitly with\n   `register-integer-module`, and Yin source reaches the exports as\n   `integer/add`, `integer/parse` and so on. Guest integers go through\n   these exports only; a VM's internal counters stay VM primitives.\n\n   Carriers. Every argument may be any exact-integer carrier the host has\n   (`dao.host.integer`); every integer result is in its one canonical\n   carrier: native iff within signed 64 bits on the JVM and Dart, or\n   within +/-(2^53 - 1) on JS, else the host big integer. Each kernel\n   promotes its operands to the host big integer before it operates and\n   demotes the result after, so no result ever passes through a rounded\n   or wrapped native intermediate, and equal values are `=` on every\n   host. The kernels are written once here over `dao.host.integer`;\n   the float and float-text arithmetic (`ratio->float`, `trunc-big`,\n   `float-digits`, `decimal->float`) is `dao.host.float`'s, and this\n   module keeps only the argument checks and refusals. Under a\n   `::max-bits` of 53 or more, `add`, `sub`, `mul` and `compare` first\n   try a native fast path on operands within +/-(2^53 - 1) whose result\n   is provably exact, and fall back to the big path otherwise.\n\n   Semantics, identical on every host:\n\n   - `add`, `sub`, `mul`, `neg` and `compare` are exact; `compare`\n     answers -1, 0 or 1.\n   - `quot-rem` answers `[q r]` truncated toward zero, `a = b*q + r`;\n     `floor-div-mod` answers Python's `[a // b, a % b]`: the quotient\n     rounds toward negative infinity and a non-zero remainder takes the\n     divisor's sign.\n   - `bit-and`, `bit-or`, `bit-xor` and `bit-not` act on infinite signed\n     two's complement, so `(bit-not a)` is `-a - 1`. `shift-left` is\n     `a * 2^k` and `shift-right` is `floor(a / 2^k)`; the count is any\n     exact integer and is never narrowed before it is checked, and a\n     right shift past the operand's length answers 0 or -1 without\n     building `2^k`.\n   - `bit-length` is the bit length of the absolute value, 0 for zero.\n   - The float exports (version 3) take and answer host binary64s: a JVM\n     `Double`, a Dart `double`, a JS `Number`. `to-float` is CPython's\n     `float(int)`, nearest with ties to even; `true-div` is CPython's\n     `int / int`, the exact ratio correctly rounded however large both\n     operands are, subnormal results included and a zero quotient\n     signed by the operands; a result that is not finite answers\n     `::float-overflow`. `compare-float` compares an integer with a\n     float exactly, -1, 0 or 1, never rounding the integer: the caller\n     excludes NaN, and an infinity compares by its sign. `from-float`\n     truncates a finite float toward zero, exactly. Each is one\n     algorithm over the shim, never a host conversion of a big integer.\n   - The float text exports (version 4) use no host float parser or\n     printer. `float-digits` answers `[digits exp10]` for a finite\n     nonzero float `v`: `digits` is the shortest decimal string, no\n     leading or trailing zero, that reads back to `v`, the closest to\n     `v` among the shortest (CPython's `repr` digits), and |v| is\n     d1.d2...dn x 10^exp10; zero, NaN and infinity are the caller's to\n     exclude. `decimal->float` is CPython's `float(str)` after syntax:\n     `digits x 10^exp10` for one or more ASCII digits and any exact\n     integer exponent, rounded once to nearest with ties to even and\n     non-negative; it answers `+inf` past the range and `0.0` below it,\n     never a limit reason, and `::max-digits` never applies to its text.\n     `max-digits` answers the composition's `::max-digits`.\n   - `pow` raises to a non-negative exponent exactly, by squaring.\n   - `parse` reads an optional `-` and one or more digits of `radix` (2\n     to 36, default 10, either letter case) exactly, never through a\n     float or a native-width parser. Signs other than `-`, prefixes,\n     underscores and whitespace are the prelude's syntax, not this\n     kernel's. `format` writes exact lowercase text, a `-` only when\n     negative, no prefix.\n\n   Limits (ruling 11) are explicit composition data with no default, in\n   bits and digits: `::max-bits` bounds the bit length of every integer\n   result, and `::max-digits` bounds the digit count `parse` reads and\n   `format` writes in a radix that is not a power of two. A result that\n   would breach `::max-bits` is refused before it is built wherever its\n   size is known in advance (`mul`, `shift-left`, `pow`).\n\n   A limit breach is returned, not thrown (version 2): the call answers\n   the keyword `::bit-limit` or `::digit-limit` in place of its result,\n   and a float result that is not finite answers `::float-overflow`\n   (version 3). Every other result is an integer, a float, a boolean, a\n   string or a pair, so the sum needs no tag, and a pair is never\n   answered with one half refused. A guest prelude translates the three\n   reasons into catchable guest exceptions; the Python profile maps\n   `::bit-limit` to `MemoryError` and `::digit-limit` to `ValueError`,\n   never `OverflowError` (`py/int-result`), and its translator must\n   learn `::float-overflow`, which is CPython's `OverflowError`, \\\"int too\n   large to convert to float\\\".\n\n   Every other refusal throws `ex-info` with the message\n   `refusal-message` and ex-data built here, never host exception text,\n   never an operand: `::op`, `::reason` and the reason's own keys. These\n   reasons are `:arity`, `:wrong-type`, `:out-of-range`, `:syntax`,\n   `:zero-division`, `:negative-count` and `:negative-exponent`: a\n   caller checks for them before the call, so reaching one is a defect\n   of the caller, and it stays a host failure, never a guest\n   exception.\"\n  (:require\n    [clojure.string :as str]\n   reference, or a non-reference. Never fatal, never mutating, and the\n   engine learns no frontend semantics from the slot. Not a guest surface:\n   the name is compiler-private (`dsg__`).\"\n  [candidate]\n  (make-effect :cell/provenance {:cell candidate}))\n\n\n(def cell-module\n  \"The `cell` module definition. Registering it is a composition step. The\n   two `dsg__` bindings are the compiler-private provenance mint and reader:\n   guest code may neither spell nor construct their names.\"\n  {'new new-cell, 'get get-cell, 'set! set-cell!, 'authentic? authentic-cell?,\n   'dsg__new-provenance new-provenance-cell,\n   'dsg__provenance provenance-cell})\n\n\n(def cell-profiles\n  \"UCF 7.5.2 profiles for the `cell` module's bindings: each an\n   `:effectful` pure effect constructor declaring its one effect kind.\"\n  {'new (vm/primitive-profile 'new :effectful [1] #{:cell/new} :none)\n   'get (vm/primitive-profile 'get :effectful [1] #{:cell/get} :none)\n   'set! (vm/primitive-profile 'set! :effectful [2] #{:cell/set!} :none)\n   'authentic? (vm/primitive-profile 'authentic? :effectful [1]\n                                     #{:cell/authentic?} :none)\n   'dsg__new-provenance (vm/primitive-profile 'dsg__new-provenance\n                                              :effectful [2]\n                                              #{:cell/new-provenance} :none)\n   'dsg__provenance (vm/primitive-profile 'dsg__provenance\n                                          :effectful [1]\n                                          #{:cell/provenance} :none)})\n\n\n(defn register-cell-module\n  \"Return registry with the `cell` module registered.\"\n    [yang.core.call :as call]\n    [yin.vm.integer :as integer]))\n\n\n#?(:clj\n   (defmacro defquoted\n     \"`def` of the quoted data `form`. ClojureDart compiles quoted data to\n      straight-line Dart, which the Dart AOT compiler cannot take as one\n      initializer, so there the data is one EDN string read back at load.\"\n     [name doc form]\n     (if (find-ns 'cljd.compiler)\n       (list 'def name doc (list 'edn/read-string (pr-str (second form))))\n       (list 'def name doc form))))\n\n\n(def host-names\n  \"Module exports the prelude calls.\"\n  (into '#{cell/new cell/get cell/set! data/count data/into data/subvec\n           data/number? data/nth data/str-concat data/str-length data/substring\n           data/str->code-points data/code-points->str data/float64\n           data/float-value data/content= data/str-unit-length data/str-unit-at\n(ns ^{:clj-kondo/config '{:lint-as {yang.php.antlr.quoted/defquoted clojure.core/def}}}\n yang.php.antlr.quoted\n  \"The `defquoted` macro the prelude's definition vectors share.\"\n  #?(:cljs (:require-macros [yang.php.antlr.quoted :refer [defquoted]]))\n  #?(:cljd (:require [clojure.edn :as edn])))\n\n\n#?(:clj\n   (defmacro defquoted\n     \"`def` of the quoted data `form`; ClojureDart reads it back from one EDN\n      string (its quoted data compiles to straight-line Dart).\"\n     [name doc form]\n     (if (find-ns 'cljd.compiler)\n       (list 'def name doc (list 'edn/read-string (pr-str (second form))))\n       (list 'def name doc form))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
